ed the whole 1099-byte string **correctly**, so the harness\\ncompared the oracle's truncated 1023-byte prefix against the port's full result\\nand reported a false divergence. Any program whose completion value exceeds the\\nbuffer was a latent false positive of this class.\\n\\n**Fix (minimal, oracle-side).**\\n- Grow `ENDOR_RESULT_MAX` to **16 KiB** so realistic completion values compare\\n  in full instead of at a 1 KiB cliff.\\n- Record the untruncated byte length (`result_len`) at both capture sites and\\n  surface `OracleOutcome::result_truncated`. When a value still overflows the\\n  buffer, `differential_check_with_symbols` / `differential_check_result_only`\\n  **skip honestly** (like an out-of-subset program) instead of comparing a\\n  truncated prefix — so the class can never spuriously diverge again, at any\\n  size.\\n\\n**Regressions.**\\n- `ironhorse-fuzz`: replays the exact 4-byte input through the real differential\\n  check and asserts **no divergence** (load-bearing — fails if the oracle fix is\\n  reverted).\\n- `xs-oracle`: a 2000-char completion value is captured **untruncated** and not\\n  flagged truncated.\\n- `ironhorse-vm`: replays the finding's program as frozen bytecode+symbols\\n  (**no XS oracle / no `c/moddable` submodule**) and asserts the port renders\\n  the whole 1099-byte result without panicking or truncating.\\n\\n**Note (latent twin).** `ModuleRunOutcome` shares the same fixed result buffer;\\nthe C side now records `result_len` on that path too, but the module differential\\ntargets do not yet consult a `result_truncated` flag. Out of scope for this\\nfinding; worth a follow-up if a module fuzz target ever surfaces a >16 KiB result.\\n\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Finding `d99d263fcf6ca7a7` — target `differential_source`\\n\\n**Symptom.** The 5-byte minimized input `2d 57 27 48 86` (sha256\\n`749f2021…e09a4f9`) folds, via the structure-aware generator, into the\\narithmetic program\\n\\n```js\\n((729808896 && (729808896 && (-83 && 327155712)))\\n * (((-83 && 327155712) * (729808896 % 603979776)) % 729808896))\\n```\\n\\nThe target panicked with a *result* divergence:\\n`oracle=\\\"57632001481506816\\\"` vs `ironhorse=\\\"57632001481506820\\\"`.\\n\\n**Root cause (harness-side, not a port defect).** In IEEE-754 doubles\\n(ECMAScript Number semantics) the program evaluates to\\n`327155712 * 176160768` = `819 · 2^46`, the **exactly-representable** double\\nwhose real value is `57632001481506816`. Both engines compute the *identical*\\ndouble — they differ only in how they render it to a string:\\n\\n- XS's `fx_dtoa` (xsdtoa.c) prints the double's exact integer,\\n  `57632001481506816` (17 significant digits).\\n- ironhorse — like V8/SpiderMonkey and ECMA-262 §6.1.6.1.20's Number\\n  `toString` (\\\"let *k* be as small as possible\\\") — prints the **shortest**\\n  round-tripping decimal, `57632001481506820` (16 significant digits).\\n\\nBoth spellings parse back to the same double, so the port was never wrong;\\nironhorse is the spec-conformant one (verified against Node/V8, which prints\\n`57632001481506820`). The harness compared the raw completion strings\\nbyte-for-byte and read XS's non-shortest rendering as a value divergence — the\\nsame *harness-artifact, not engine bug* shape as findings `493390fc03979205`\\nand `5d122a6fc10babd9`.\\n\\n**Fix (minimal, harness-side).** Compare **Number** completions by their\\nIEEE-754 double rather than their decimal spelling. A new `results_agree`\\nhelper treats two result strings as equal when they are byte-identical **or**\\nboth parse as the same finite `f64`; the three differential checks\\n(`differential_check`, `…_with_symbols`, `…_result_only`) call it at their\\nresult-comparison site. Two *different* doubles never share a parse, so every\\ngenuine value divergence is still caught; only differently-spelled renderings\\nof the *same* double are suppressed. Non-numeric completions (booleans,\\nstrings, `Infinity`/`NaN`) fall through to the byte comparison unchanged.\\n\\n**Regressions.**\\n- `ironhorse-fuzz`: replays the exact 5-byte input through the real\\n  `differential_check` and asserts **no divergence** (load-bearing — fails if\\n  the fix is reverted), plus a `results_agree` unit test pinning the\\n  same-double / different-double boundary.\\n- `ironhorse-vm` (`finding_d99d263fcf6ca7a7_large_integer_dtoa`): pins the\\n  port's `number_to_ecma_string` for the reproducing value to the shortest\\n  round-tripping decimal `57632001481506820` (matching V8, not XS's exact\\n  integer). Builds **without the XS oracle or the `c/moddable` submodule**, and\\n  is the crate CI actually runs (`cargo test -p ironhorse-vm`).\\n\",\"headRefName\":\"ironhorse-fuzz-findings\",\"headRefOid\":\"13ae6c6f7b18cc405260eeb5e53a163988a4f3f4\",\"headRepositoryOwner\":{\"id\":\"U_kgDOEKJuwA\",\"name\":\"Kriscendo Bot\",\"login\":\"kriscendobot\"},\"isDraft\":true,\"number\":1088,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"title\":\"Ironhorse fuzz findings: regressions + fixes (standing)\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/endojs/endo-but-for-bots/pull/1088\"}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
